  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1025 PM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nAn UL ridge over AZ and an UL trough over the Southern Plains \nhave left us in NW'LY flow aloft with relatively high pressure at \nthe surface. Winds are light. Skies are clear, and the air is \ndrying. This pattern will set the stage for fairly efficient \nradiational cooling with a widespread freeze expected for much of \nthe CWA. These temperatures will likely result in the first freeze\nfor Las Cruces and low-lying and less urban areas of El Paso. The\nairport will get close to freezing, but may fall just short of \nthe 32 degree mark.\n\nThe UL ridge will slide east, centering overhead by tomorrow. By\nThanksgiving, a s/w trough will slide southeastward along the\nUS/Mex Pacific coast, keeping any influence from it too far to \nour south and west. The net pattern will be an UL trough \noverhead, but to little consequence. An UL ridge will very slowly\nreplace said trough by the weekend, before going zonal aloft by\nMonday. This will continue our stretch of dry weather with mainly\nlight winds while temperatures warm well above normal (which is \nupper 60s to lower 70s). A weak cold front may push in Sunday to \nlower highs closer to normal, and winds may be a little breezier \non Monday if the GFS verifies.",
